Basic debugging

1. `Basic Print Statements` --> Add print statements to check what is unexpected going.

2. `-ggdb -fsanitize=address,undefined ` --> **When to use it:** :
	1.  During active development and testing.
	2. Whenever your program crashes mysteriously (e.g., a "Segmentation fault").
	3. **Never** use it in your final, released version (it slows down the program 
	and uses extra memory).
	4. **How to use it:** :-
		1. **Open VS Code Terminal:** Press `` Ctrl + ` `` (backtick) or go to **Terminal > 
		New Terminal** in the top menu. Ensure the terminal path matches the folder where your
		C++ file is saved.
		2. Type the following command and press Enter: 
			 `g++ -ggdb -fsanitize=address,undefined your_file.cpp -o my_program`
		3. *(Replace `your_file.cpp` with the actual name of your file).* 

3. `Possible Pitfall: Rewriting the Program` --> Rewriting the program is sometimes a good idea, 
    but remember to keep track of time! It is very easy to get carried away rewriting even parts
    of a program that are bug-free in obsessive hope that something might come through. Do not
    delete your program. Make a new file!
    
4. `Debugging tactic 
	1. `#1: Commenting out your code` -->  Let’s start with an easy one. If your program is exhibiting 
	erroneous behavior, one way to reduce the amount of code you have to search through is to comment
	some code out and see if the issue persists. If the issue remains unchanged, the commented out code 
	probably wasn’t responsible.
		1. `Warning` --> Don’t forget which functions you’ve commented out so you can uncomment them 
		later! After making many debugging-related changes, it’s very easy to miss undoing one or two. 
		If that happens, you’ll end up fixing one bug but introducing others! Having a good version control 
		system is extremely useful here, as you can diff your code against the main branch to see all the 
		debugging-related changes you’ve made (and ensure that they are reverted before you commit your change).
		
5. `If string consists of multiple chunks and wanted to do certain operation on different chunks` -->  
    `std::stringstream ss(s); while (ss >> s) {ans += s[0];}` if string s has "Hello world" we converted 
    this  for taking chunks and then stored first character of each chunk .and inside this while loop you write
    `cout << s.size()` then you will get size of current chunk of string.Means any operation inside the while loop
    while be done specifically on that particular current chunk only
    
6. `cin.ignore()` --> Use `cin.ignore()` whenever you use `getline()` **immediately after** `cin >>`
    (this cin is before getline).
    
7.  `vector <string> words;` --> by this you created a vector of string and this will be useful when 
    you need indices of chunks of words in a long string and without spaces as:
	1.  `stringstream a(s); vector <string> word; while(a >> s){ word.push_back(s);}`
